  "reply_markdown": "**Q1. The claimed invariants do not all hold.** Presets, templates, coordinator-owned statuses, normal structured ingestion, and the placement of the four final review blocks are implemented. All three scripts parsed successfully on the installed PowerShell 5.1.\n\nThe remaining defects are detailed individually below:\n\n- JSON persistence overwrites the existing stores directly; interrupted writes can destroy previously committed history.\n- Stale-lock reclamation checks and deletes in separate operations, allowing two contenders to acquire ownership.\n- Timeout handling releases the lock even when descendants survive; a dead bridge PID also does not establish that its Codex child stopped.\n- The fingerprint omits file modes, and its blob lookup conflates case-distinct paths on case-sensitive filesystems.\n- Briefs and artifacts are hashed only before the run; changes to these excluded inputs can escape the after-review check.\n- Failure to preserve the raw JSON companion merely prints a warning, then permits success and deletes the temporary original.\n\n**Q2. Validation has three concrete gaps.** First, a reply can say `ACCEPT`, introduce no new findings, and report an existing blocker as `still-open`. The bridge records ACCEPT and renders “Blockers: none.” This is especially consequential because the prompt correctly discourages refiling unchanged findings. Contextual validation must join prior IDs to their existing severities.\n\nSecond, `locations[].line: 1e30` passes integer validation but overflows during normalization. The exception occurs before durable reply preservation, and cleanup deletes the temporary response. This must become recorded validation failure, not lost evidence.\n\nThird, purpose-specific verdict restrictions exist only in the prompt: acceptance can record `ADVISE`. Validate the verdict against the resolved purpose.\n\nMemory-only probes reproduced the prior-blocker contradiction and overflow. Normal omitted IDs become `not-checked`, unknown IDs create no findings, and reviewer checks do not automatically change coordinator status.\n\n**Q3. Write order is insufficient recovery.** Three concrete sequences remain:\n\n1. Kill the process after `WriteAllText` truncates either JSON store. Historical records are lost or the file becomes unparseable; an empty findings file is silently treated as a new store.\n2. Two contenders both finish checking the same stale lock. One deletes it and creates its own lock; the second then deletes that replacement and creates another. Both proceed from independently loaded state, risking overwritten replies and lost ledger updates.\n3. A consultation writes only reviewer checks, then crashes before writing sessions. `-List` checks each finding’s original `source.consult`, so it reports no orphan. Number allocation also ignores `reviewer_checks[].consult`, allowing the next consultation to reuse that number and misattribute the old check. Raw mode additionally ignores finding-source reservations.\n\nAtomic replacement is needed for each store, plus explicit recovery accounting for every consultation and audit reference. A full database is unnecessary, but these guarantees cannot be replaced by filename numbering.\n\n**Q4. HOLD.** The four blockers are destructive persistence, stale-lock concurrency, incomplete recovery accounting, and ACCEPT bypassing prior blockers. The other findings identify additional release defects.\n\n**Unproven scenarios:** I did not run a writing harness, crash injection, or process termination tests. PowerShell 7 and non-Windows behavior remain unverified. This consultation’s final persistence cannot be observed before this response returns. Two read-only fingerprint samples differed (`29b6d98552e55…` and `1ab7ab749142…`), so this review also lacks a stable source snapshot.\n\n**Observable first-run checklist:** After this consultation finishes, verify the three `04-codex-acceptance-0.2` artifacts, the final event and thread identity, and the appended ledger entry. It must record `structured=true`, empty `validation_error`, `verdict=HOLD`, four blockers, seven majors, and eleven finding IDs `F04-1` through `F04-11`, matching eleven proposed records and their evidence. Confirm the Markdown ends with Verdict, Blockers, Unproven scenarios, and First-run checklist; verify revision/brief hashes, drift reporting, usage, and lock removal. Exit code 0 would establish successful delivery of HOLD, not release acceptance.",
